- # Apex LWC Controller Best Practices

  This guide outlines standards for Apex classes that serve as backends for Lightning Web Components. It ensures your LWC controllers are secure, performant, and aligned with Salesforce's client-server architecture.

- ## 1. General Structure

  - Place controller classes in a dedicated namespace like `lwc`, `api`, or `ui`.
  - Use a naming convention like `MyComponentController.cls` to match the related component.

  ```apex
  public with sharing class MyComponentController {
      @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)
      public static List<Account> getAccounts() {
          // logic
      }
  }
  ```

2. Required Annotations
Use @AuraEnabled to expose methods or properties.

Use cacheable=true for read-only methods that can be cached on the client.

Avoid @AuraEnabled without static unless necessary.

Only expose the minimum required methods.

Avoid exposing entire SObject types unless necessary — prefer DTOs (Data Transfer Objects).

3. CRUD and FLS Enforcement
Always enforce CRUD and FLS before performing any DML or returning SObject fields.
```apex
Schema.SObjectField[] fields = new List<Schema.SObjectField>{
    Account.Name, Account.Industry
};
SObjectAccessDecision decision = Security.stripInaccessible(AccessType.READABLE, accounts, fields);
```

Never return fields that a user doesn’t have permission to view.

4. Governor Limits and Bulk Safety
Avoid DML or SOQL inside loops.

Use maps, collections, and pre-aggregation where possible.

Respect limits like:

100 SOQL queries per transaction

150 DML statements

CPU time

5. Security Best Practices
with sharing: Use with sharing unless there’s a clear need for system-level access.

Avoid exposing internal logic or sensitive metadata.

Input validation: Validate all parameters passed from the client.
```apex
if (String.isBlank(inputName)) {
    throw new AuraHandledException('Invalid input');
}
```
Do not expose raw errors to the client; wrap with AuraHandledException where needed.

6. Return Types and Serialization
Use lightweight DTOs instead of returning entire SObjects when possible.

Ensure returned data is JSON-serializable (e.g., no circular references).

Do not expose sensitive fields in return objects.

7. Caching and Performance
Use @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) where applicable for read-only methods.

Avoid unnecessary queries and logic for UI-invoked methods.

Leverage @wire with cacheable methods to improve LWC performance.

8. Naming and Documentation
Name methods clearly based on their action (getOpenCases, createTaskForUser).

Use comments to describe purpose, expected inputs, and security constraints.

Group related logic into cohesive classes. Avoid massive multipurpose controllers.

9. Testing
Write unit tests for each exposed method, testing both valid and invalid scenarios.

Use @testSetup to create data.

Assert returned results and security filters.
```apex
@isTest
private class MyComponentControllerTest {
    @isTest static void testGetAccounts() {
        Test.startTest();
        List<Account> result = MyComponentController.getAccounts();
        Test.stopTest();

        System.assertNotEquals(null, result);
        System.assert(!result.isEmpty());
    }
}
```
10. Common Pitfalls
Returning too much data to the client.

Not filtering based on user access.

Failing to enforce FLS or CRUD checks.

No @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) when applicable.

Using without sharing without documented justification.

By following these guidelines, your LWC controllers will be scalable, secure, and performant while offering a consistent and maintainable API to front-end components.
